<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emil River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Emil is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> that rises in the Tarbagatai region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and flows west into Lake Alakol in Kazakhstan. Its valley and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> were the pastures given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> to his son Ögedei. The region remained the heartland of the Ögedeid line until its lands were absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> in the early 14th century.</p></div>
